From 83d9ca9fbf6028574377c320b1ecb95f583a44c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Okie14 <212424299+mohammad-vivian-SPOOKY@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:15:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(state): stamp the full login subject so issuer-bearing
 logins can reclaim their own rows

Desktop/dashboard/serve logins stamped unrouted legacy session rows as the lossy
<provider>:<user> form (tui_gateway/session_transports.py::_transport_auth_user_id),
dropping the issuer entirely. _login_label (hermes_state_mutation_binding.py) then
refused to derive that same label from a live request's full auth:v1:[provider,
issuer, user] subject whenever an issuer was present -- which every real OIDC login
(self-hosted or Nous) carries. The practical effect: an OIDC-authenticated user could
never reclaim/rename/resume a row their own login created, on any surface.

Fix: stamp new rows with the full subject directly (gateway.session_identity's
authenticated_subject -- the same serialization live authorization already compares
against), instead of a second, lossy encoding of the same identity. authorize_history's
exact-subject match now succeeds for new rows without needing _login_label at all.

_login_label is unchanged and still correct for its original, narrower scope: an
issuer-less login's row. Rows already stamped under the old lossy form by an
issuer-bearing login are handled by a new, deliberately bounded legacy fallback
(_legacy_transport_label / _legacy_label_eligible) that only resolves a row provably
created before this host's recorded cutover to full-subject stamping (a new
state_meta marker, written once by the SCHEMA_VERSION 33 migration on an upgrading
store, or at creation time on a fresh one). The first genuine owner to touch such a
row post-upgrade gets it re-stamped in place with the full subject -- no admin step,
no bulk migration, no window where a row sits unowned -- and the fallback can never
reopen later even if a second issuer is added behind the same provider name, since
it is bounded by creation time, not just by format.

Also fixes the agent-construction side: _transport_auth_user_id's output also feeds
the user_id passed to AIAgent for memory/personalization scoping
(tui_gateway/server.py::_make_agent), so this closes the same issuer-dropping gap
there too -- two OIDC 'alice's from different issuers now stay distinct memory peers,
matching the existing basic-vs-OIDC disambiguation the short label already did.

Explicitly out of scope: multi-tenant access control (#84743, closed not-planned;
the real contract today is single-owner-per-profile, with #79498/#70867 pointing at
profile-level routing as the intended direction for multiple tenants) and local
CLI/TUI identity (gateway/control_socket.py's uid:<uid> peer-credential has no real
per-human identity story today -- a separate, deeper question already surfacing in
#143907).

Tests: 5 new cases in tests/hermes_state/test_history_login_label.py covering the
legacy-fallback upgrade, a foreign-user refusal under the fallback, the post-cutover
bound actually holding, and fail-closed behavior with no cutover marker. Updated
hardcoded old-format expectations in tests/tui_gateway/test_desktop_session_login_identity.py
and tests/tui_gateway/test_tui_gateway_server.py to the new full-subject form.

Verified: 21/21 in the core login-label suite (16 pre-existing regression-safe +
5 new), 638/638 in the full tui_gateway server suite, 294/294 in the full
hermes_state suite, plus every other test file referencing the touched functions
passing individually.

